    "evidence_text": "The mice were initially generated using the CAG promoter; lines expressing the G(1) and S/G(2)/M phase probes that emitted orange (mKO2) and green (mAG) fluorescence, respectively, were separately constructed.\n\nFinally, we show that the ectopic expression of Prox1 induces premature differentiation of neural stem cells.\n\nHowever, NPCs express Sox2 endogenously, possibly facilitating reprogramming in the absence of exogenous Sox2.\n\nThis study demonstrates that small molecules identified through a phenotypic screen can compensate for viral transduction of critical factors, such as Sox2, and improve reprogramming efficiency.\n\nTo characterize the properties of adult neural stem cells (NSCs), we generated and analyzed Sox2-GFP transgenic mice.\n\nSox2-GFP cells in the subgranular zone (SGZ) express markers specific for progenitors, but they represent two morphologically distinct populations that differ in proliferation levels.\n\nTo aid in the investigation of these issues, we developed strains of transgenic mice in which a green fluorescent protein (GFP) is expressed in the ureteric bud under the control of the Hoxb7 promoter.",
